    "alert_id": "ALERT_001",
    "title": "Rising creatinine on TDF in pregnancy",
    "message": "TDF is associated with nephrotoxicity including proximal tubular dysfunction and declining eGFR. Patient's creatinine rose from 0.8 to 1.1 mg/dL within 10 weeks of starting TDF, with eGFR now 78 mL/min/1.73m². Pregnancy normally increases GFR, so creatinine should trend downward, making this rise more concerning. TDF is the likely contributor. However, pregnancy-related physiological changes or pre-existing renal issues could also play a role. Phosphate remains normal (3.8 mg/dL), which argues against significant proximal tubular dysfunction. Continued monitoring is essential given the compounding of TDF nephrotoxicity risk with pregnancy.",
    "issue_type": "toxicity",
    "severity": "moderate",
    "recommended_action": "Monitor renal function and phosphate closely. If creatinine continues to rise or eGFR declines further, consider switching TDF to ABC or AZT, especially during pregnancy.",
    "citations": []
  },
  {
    "alert_id": "ALERT_002",
    "title": "DTG–iron supplement interaction in pregnancy",
    "message": "DTG should be separated from divalent cation-containing supplements including iron by at least 2 hours, or given at a different time, as co-administration reduces DTG absorption. Patient is pregnant and prescribed ferrous sulfate. No documentation confirms whether timing separation is being followed. This interaction is particularly important in pregnancy where maintaining viral suppression is critical for PMTCT.",
    "issue_type": "drug_interaction",
    "severity": "moderate",
    "recommended_action": "Counsel patient to take DTG at least 2 hours apart from ferrous sulfate, or take iron with food and DTG on empty stomach. Document adherence to separation.",
